Clara turned to the museum staff standing near the entrance. "The thief did not break in from outside," she announced calmly. "Whoever took the painting was already inside the building before the rain started, and they used the maintenance shaft to hide the canvas." She walked straight to the janitor''s closet, opened the metal vent cover, and pulled out the rolled-up painting. Mr. Sterling''s assistant, who had muddy boots hidden under his desk, immediately confessed to the crime.', '"Whoever took the painting was already inside the building before the rain started, and they used the maintenance shaft..." The word "they" in the sentence refers to ….', '[{"key":"A","text":"Mr. Sterling and his assistant"},{"key":"B","text":"the security guards"},{"key":"C","text":"the thief"},{"key":"D","text":"the museum staff"},{"key":"E","text":"Clara and Leo"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'Read the subject "Whoever took the painting" in the first half of the same sentence.', 'Singular "they" is used right after the indefinite pronoun phrase "Whoever took the painting," meaning the unknown thief.', ARRAY['pronoun-reference', 'mystery']::text[]),
  ('ENG10-RD-008', 'Bahasa Inggris Kelas X', 'reading-narrative-recount', 'Reading: Narrative, Mystery, Historical & Recount', 'medium', 'C2', 'text', 'The Mystery of the Missing Painting

Late one rainy evening, Detective Clara Vance was called to the Blackwood Art Museum. The curator, Mr. Sterling, looked pale and anxious. "Detective, our most valuable 18th-century painting, The Crimson Rose, has vanished from the main gallery!" he exclaimed. "The security cameras were turned off at 9:00 PM, and all the doors were locked from the inside."

Clara walked into the silent gallery with her assistant, Leo. She ignored the empty frame on the wall and immediately inspected the polished marble floor. "Look at these faint muddy footprints leading toward the ventilation shaft," Clara pointed out. "And notice that the window latch is completely dry, even though it has been pouring outside since dusk."

Clara turned to the museum staff standing near the entrance. "The thief did not break in from outside," she announced calmly. "Whoever took the painting was already inside the building before the rain started, and they used the maintenance shaft to hide the canvas." She walked straight to the janitor''s closet, opened the metal vent cover, and pulled out the rolled-up painting. Mr. Sterling''s assistant, who had muddy boots hidden under his desk, immediately confessed to the crime.', 'Where was the stolen painting, The Crimson Rose, eventually found?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Hidden underneath the security camera console desk."},{"key":"B","text":"Wrapped inside a raincoat outside the garden window."},{"key":"C","text":"Placed inside Mr. Sterling''s locked office safe."},{"key":"D","text":"Suspended behind the original wooden frame in the main gallery."},{"key":"E","text":"Rolled up inside a metal vent cover in the janitor’s closet."}]'::jsonb, 'E', 'Follow Detective Clara’s final steps in the third paragraph.', 'Clara walked straight to the janitor’s closet, opened the metal vent cover, and pulled out the rolled-up painting.', ARRAY['narrative', 'mystery', 'detail']::text[]),
  ('ENG10-RD-009', 'Bahasa Inggris Kelas X', 'reading-narrative-recount', 'Reading: Narrative, Mystery, Historical & Recount', 'mudah', 'C2', 'text', 'The Rise and Fall of Machu Picchu

Machu Picchu is a 15th-century Inca citadel located high in the Andes Mountains of Peru, standing over 2,400 meters above sea level. Built around 1450 during the reign of the Inca emperor Pachacuti, the site served as a royal estate and a sacred religious center. The Inca engineers constructed the city using polished dry-stone walls without mortar, allowing the buildings to withstand frequent earthquakes in the region.

Roughly a century after its construction, Machu Picchu was abandoned around the time of the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire. Because of its remote location hidden among steep mountain ridges and dense cloud forests, Spanish conquistadors never found the citadel. As a result, Machu Picchu escaped the destruction that befell many other Inca settlements, remaining largely unknown to the outside world for centuries.

In 1911, American historian Hiram Bingham was guided to the site by local indigenous farmers, bringing Machu Picchu to international attention. Subsequent archaeological studies revealed temples, terraces, and water channels that demonstrated the advanced urban planning of the Inca civilization. Today, Machu Picchu is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and stands as one of the most iconic historical landmarks in South America.', 'What does the passage state about how Machu Picchu survived earthquakes?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Local farmers continuously repaired the cracked stone walls every year."},{"key":"B","text":"Its buildings were constructed using fitted dry-stone walls without mortar."},{"key":"C","text":"Spanish engineers rebuilt the foundations using modern cement techniques."},{"key":"D","text":"The Inca builders reinforced the roofs with heavy wooden beams."},{"key":"E","text":"The city was built inside a deep cave protected from mountain tremors."}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'Check the last sentence of the first paragraph.', 'The text states: "The Inca engineers constructed the city using polished dry-stone walls without mortar, allowing the buildings to withstand frequent earthquakes in the region."', ARRAY['historical-text', 'detail', 'machu-picchu']::text[]),
  ('ENG10-RD-010', 'Bahasa Inggris Kelas X', 'reading-narrative-recount', 'Reading: Narrative, Mystery, Historical & Recount', 'mudah', 'C2', 'text', 'The Rise and Fall of Machu Picchu

Machu Picchu is a 15th-century Inca citadel located high in the Andes Mountains of Peru, standing over 2,400 meters above sea level. Built around 1450 during the reign of the Inca emperor Pachacuti, the site served as a royal estate and a sacred religious center. The Inca engineers constructed the city using polished dry-stone walls without mortar, allowing the buildings to withstand frequent earthquakes in the region.

Roughly a century after its construction, Machu Picchu was abandoned around the time of the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire. Because of its remote location hidden among steep mountain ridges and dense cloud forests, Spanish conquistadors never found the citadel. As a result, Machu Picchu escaped the destruction that befell many other Inca settlements, remaining largely unknown to the outside world for centuries.

In 1911, American historian Hiram Bingham was guided to the site by local indigenous farmers, bringing Machu Picchu to international attention. Subsequent archaeological studies revealed temples, terraces, and water channels that demonstrated the advanced urban planning of the Inca civilization. Today, Machu Picchu is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and stands as one of the most iconic historical landmarks in South America.', 'Why was Machu Picchu spared from destruction during the Spanish conquest?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Local villagers disguised the stone temples as natural forest cliffs."},{"key":"B","text":"The Inca army successfully defended the citadel against Spanish soldiers."},{"key":"C","text":"Its isolated location in the high mountains kept it hidden from the Spanish."},{"key":"D","text":"Thick volcanic ash covered the city before the Spanish arrived in Peru."},{"key":"E","text":"The Spanish conquistadors decided to preserve it as a royal palace."}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'Read the second paragraph explaining why Spanish conquistadors never found it.', 'Because of its remote location hidden among steep mountain ridges and dense cloud forests, Spanish conquistadors never found or destroyed Machu Picchu.', ARRAY['historical-text', 'cause-effect', 'machu-picchu']::text[]),
  ('ENG10-RD-011', 'Bahasa Inggris Kelas X', 'reading-narrative-recount', 'Reading: Narrative, Mystery, Historical & Recount', 'mudah', 'C1', 'text', 'The Rise and Fall of Machu Picchu

